        <ArticleTitle>Intensive versus standard blood-pressure control in adults.</ArticleTitle>
        <Pagination><MedlinePgn>1091-1103</MedlinePgn></Pagination>
        <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1056/NEJMoa1234567</ELocationID>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Elevated systolic blood pressure remains a leading modifiable contributor to cardiovascular disease worldwide. The optimal target for treatment has been debated for two decades.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We randomly assigned 9361 participants with elevated systolic blood pressure to a target of less than 120 mm Hg or less than 140 mm Hg. Participants were followed for a median of 3.26 years. The primary outcome was a composite of myocardial infarction, stroke, and death from cardiovascular causes.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The primary outcome occurred in 243 participants in the intensive-treatment group and 319 in the standard-treatment group. Serious adverse events of hypotension occurred more frequently in the intensive-treatment group.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Targeting a systolic pressure below 120 mm Hg reduced rates of major cardiovascular events, although rates of some adverse events were higher. See <i>Supplementary Appendix</i> for full outcome definitions.</AbstractText>
        </Abstract>
